course: biology
lesson: 101
title: The African savanna area: giraffes, zebras, rhinos and antelopes
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: At the zoo the African savanna area is one big grassy paddock where giraffes, zebras and antelopes wander together, just as they share the plains of Africa, while the lions live next door behind a strong fence.
explain: Each animal has its own way of living on the savanna: the giraffe eats leaves high up where nobody else reaches, the zebra and the white rhino graze the grass, and antelopes nibble and run.
explain: Picture a giraffe drinking: it has to spread its front legs wide like a clumsy tripod to reach the water, and that is the moment a lion likes best, so in the wild giraffes drink only now and then.
explain: Cartoons such as Madagascar make the zebra and the giraffe chatty friends of a lion, but on the real savanna a lion is the animal they watch for all day, and even a giraffe's neck has only seven bones, the same as yours.
fact: Zoo giraffes feed from baskets and nets hung high, as wild giraffes feed from tall acacia trees, and a giraffe's neck has seven very long bones, called vertebrae, the same number as a person's neck.
fact: A giraffe's tongue is about 45 to 50 centimetres long and dark blue-black; it pulls leaves off thorny acacias, thick sticky spit protects it from thorns, and the dark colour may help against sunburn.
fact: A giraffe must spread its front legs wide to drink, because its neck cannot reach the ground with straight legs, and special valves in its neck stop blood rushing to its head when it bends.
fact: Zebras often rest side by side facing different ways, so many eyes watch in every direction for lions, and scientists have found that stripes seem to confuse biting flies.
fact: Zebra skin is dark under the hair, so scientists usually describe a zebra as black with white stripes, and every zebra has its own stripe pattern, which helps a foal know its mother.
fact: The white rhino has a wide, square mouth for grazing grass and the black rhino a pointed, hooked lip for picking leaves; both are grey, and «white» may come from a word meaning «wide».
fact: Rhino horn is made of keratin, the same stuff as hair and fingernails; it is no medicine, but people who wrongly think it is pay poachers who kill rhinos for it.
fact: Poaching and war killed nearly all northern white rhinos; the last male, Sudan, died in 2018, so only two females are left, and scientists are trying to make new calves from frozen cells with southern white rhinos as mothers.
fact: Springboks and some gazelles leap high with stiff legs, called pronking or stotting, which seems to tell a predator «I am fit, don't bother» and warns the rest of the herd.
fact: Zoos can keep giraffes, zebras and antelopes in one paddock because they eat different plants and do not hunt each other, while lions are kept separately next door.
fact: Giraffes sleep only a few hours a day in short naps, sometimes lying down with the head curled back on the body, and in the wild they mostly rest standing up because lying down is risky.
fact: A baby giraffe is born by dropping about 2 metres to the ground, which is normal and safe for it, and it can stand within about an hour.
exercise: How many bones are in a giraffe's long neck? | Seven, the same as in a person's neck | About seventy, one for each hand's width | Twenty, many more than a person has | Just one, very long, bendy bone
exercise: Why do zoos hang giraffe food in high baskets? | Giraffes feed high up in tall trees in the wild | Giraffes cannot bend their long necks down at all | It keeps the food away from the rain | Giraffes only eat what they see moving
exercise: What colour is a giraffe's tongue? | Dark blue-black | Bright pink like a dog's | Spotted like its coat | Pale yellow like straw
exercise: How does a giraffe's tongue cope with sharp acacia thorns? | Thick sticky spit coats the thorns | Its tongue is made of hard bone | It only eats trees with no thorns | It pulls the thorns out with its teeth first
exercise: Why does a giraffe spread its front legs to drink? | Its neck cannot reach the water with straight legs | It is getting ready to run away | It wants to look much bigger to any lions that are watching | Its knees cannot bend at all
exercise: Why do zebras often rest facing different ways? | So eyes watch every direction | So their stripes line up into one pattern | So the wind does not blow their manes | So each zebra keeps its own patch of grass
exercise: What do scientists think stripes do best for a zebra? | They confuse biting flies | They make the zebra run much faster | They keep the zebra's skin wet | They help it climb up trees
exercise: Is a zebra white with black stripes or black with white stripes? | Black with white stripes, as its skin is dark | White with black stripes, as its skin is pink | It has no skin colour at all under the hair | It changes colour between day and night
exercise: How can a zebra foal find its mother in a big herd? | Each zebra has its own stripes | All the mothers have the very same stripes | Mothers wear a special smell collar | Foals follow whichever zebra is tallest
exercise: How can you tell a white rhino from a black rhino? | The white rhino has a wide square mouth for grass | The white rhino is painted white by keepers | The black rhino has no horn at all, only a bump on its nose | The white rhino lives only in the snow
exercise: What is rhino horn made of? | Keratin, like hair and fingernails | Ivory, like an elephant's tusk | Solid bone, like a deer's antler | Hard wood that grows on the skin
exercise: Why are only two northern white rhinos left? | Poaching and war killed the rest | They all moved to live in zoos | They turned into southern white rhinos | Lions hunted every male
exercise: Why does a springbok pronk when a predator is near? | To show it is fit and not worth chasing | To reach the juicy leaves on high branches | To shake off ticks and flies | To call its baby back to the herd
exercise: Why can giraffes, zebras and antelopes share one paddock? | They eat different plants and do not hunt each other | They are all the same kind of animal | The keepers trained them all to be friends since birth | They sleep at different times of year
exercise: How does a giraffe sometimes sleep when it lies down? | With its head curled back | With its neck stretched flat on the ground | Hanging its head over a branch | Standing on three legs with eyes open
exercise: Why do wild giraffes sleep only a few hours a day? | Lying down makes them easy prey for lions | Their long necks stop them breathing lying down | They are too busy eating at night | Giraffes do not need any sleep at all
test: 3 giraffes and 4 zebras in the paddock, how many legs altogether? | 28 | 24 | 21 | 32
test: «zebras are white horses with black stripes painted on, innit» What is right? | No, the skin is dark, so it is black with white stripes | Yes, the stripes are just dye in the hair | Yes, and the stripes wash right off in heavy rain | No, zebras are grey under all the hair
test: In the film Madagascar the zebra and the giraffe are best mates with a lion. What is real? | On the savanna zebras and giraffes are prey and watch for lions | Lions only eat grass, so they are safe friends for everyone | Giraffes are too tall for lions to notice | Zebras and lions always share one waterhole calmly
test: My mate says giraffes have loads more neck bones than us. Right? | No, both have seven, but a giraffe's are very long | Yes, a giraffe has about fifty neck bones | Yes, it has one extra neck bone for each metre of its neck | No, a giraffe has only three neck bones
test: «gonna grind up some rhino horn, it's medicine» What is true? | It is keratin like your nails and cures nothing | It is a strong medicine that doctors use for fevers and pain | It works only if the rhino was a white one | It is safe medicine because horns grow back
test: What would happen if the zoo put the lions in with the zebras? | The lions would hunt them and the zebras would panic | They would share the grass peacefully | The zebras would chase the lions away | Nothing, because zoo lions quickly forget how to hunt at all
test: «the white rhino's not even white, someone's having us on» Why the name? | It may come from a word meaning «wide», for its mouth | It turns white in the winter like an Arctic fox | Babies are born white and go grey | It rolls in white mud every day
test: Why is a waterhole a dangerous place for a giraffe? | It must bend down awkwardly and cannot run quickly | The water is too cold for its long legs | Crocodiles climb up trees to catch giraffes as they drink | Its neck sinks into the mud
test: A herd of antelopes suddenly springs straight up for no reason. What is going on? | They are pronking, showing a predator they are fit | They are dancing because they are happy | They are trying to reach the high leaves on the trees | They are jumping over invisible fences
test: How many ears do 5 giraffes and 2 rhinos have altogether? | 14 | 7 | 12 | 10
test: Why are scientists freezing cells of the northern white rhino? | To try to make new calves after the last male died | To make rhino horn for medicine | To keep the cells safe as special food for zoo animals | To turn them into black rhinos
test: «a baby giraffe falls 2 metres when it's born?! poor thing» What is true? | Yes, and the drop is normal and safe for it | No, giraffes are born lying in a nest | Yes, and most babies are hurt by it | No, giraffes lay eggs in tall trees
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the neck of the giraffe? | It comprises seven vertebrae, each of exceptional length, as in the human neck | It comprises some seventy vertebrae of ordinary length | It consists of a single elongated bone | It comprises twenty vertebrae, considerably more than are found in the neck of man
exam: Identify the principal reason for which zebras at rest are frequently observed to face in divergent directions. | That predators approaching from any direction may be detected | That their stripes may present a single continuous pattern | That each individual may defend a separate area of grass | That the wind may be prevented from disturbing the mane
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the substance of which rhinoceros horn is composed. | It is composed of keratin, the substance of hair and fingernails, and possesses no medicinal property | It is composed of ivory, like the tusk of the elephant, and is employed in modern medicine | It is composed of solid bone and is renewed annually | It is composed of a woody material of considerable medicinal value
exam: State the principal significance of the stiff-legged vertical leap termed pronking, as exhibited by springboks. | It signals to a predator that the animal is fit and not worth pursuing | It enables the animal to reach the foliage of tall trees | It serves to dislodge parasites from the coat | It constitutes a dance performed solely in fine weather
